<title>UK: UK PSBR 3.58 bln stg in Feb.</title>
<headline>UK PSBR 3.58 bln stg in Feb.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-03-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>UK public sector borrowing requirement data for February. Deficits unless noted.</p>
<p> PSBR (in million stg)		 FEB     APR-FEB    APR-FEB</p>
<p>						   1996/97    1995/96</p>
<p> Public sector		     3,580   14,093     22,216</p>
<p> Central government		5,389   17,792     26,680</p>
<p> Central government own acct   5,182   17,650     26,760</p>
<p> Local Authority		   727rep  792rep     1,177rep</p>
<p>    from central govt		128    1,520	  949</p>
<p> General Government		4,534   15,480     24,554</p>
<p> Public Corporation		875rep  2,765rep   3,367rep</p>
<p>Forecast -- consensus forecast suggests 3.7 bln stg.</p>
<p>Rep=repayment</p>
<p> GOVERNMENT RECEIPTS	     FEB	APR-FEB    APR-FEB</p>
<p>						     96/97	 95/96</p>
<p> Central govt total receipts  19,271    242,714    225,946</p>
<p> Inland revenue receipts	 7,425     96,777     90,404</p>
<p>  of which income tax	    6,318     63,624     62,510</p>
<p>  of which corporation tax	 437     27,192     23,456</p>
<p> Customs and excise		6,753     77,617     71,070</p>
<p>  of which VAT		     3,769     44,674     39,969</p>
<p> Social security		   3,632     41,175     39,209</p>
<p> Interest				545	6,696	7,220</p>
<p> Other				   916     20,449     18,043</p>
<p>CENTRAL GOVT OUTLAYS</p>
<p> Total				24,453    260,364    252,706</p>
<p>  of which interest		2,329     24,749     21,492</p>
<p> Net departmental outlays     22,143    239,804    233,510</p>
<p>Note- Privatisation proceeds in February were 19 million stg compared with 1.5 billion stg in February 1996.</p>
<p>The ONS said January's debt repayment was revised downwards by 0.1 billion stg to 5.7 billion. The March PSBR will be reduced by the 0.5 billion stg first instalment from the sale of the Housing Corporation loan book and cut by a further 0.1 billion stg by the total proceeds from the sale of the Housing for Wales loan book. Both transactions will reduce net departmental outlays, the ONS said.</p>
